WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.562
An agent's memory problem is not storage.

00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:06.452
Facts arrive connected, and get stored flat.

00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:08.550
So what if we kept the connections?

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:15.324
Vector search finds passages that sound like this. It fails on multi-hop questions.

00:00:15.424 --> 00:00:20.854
A knowledge graph keeps entities as nodes, and relationships as typed edges.

00:00:21.404 --> 00:00:27.683
GraphRAG makes the index a graph. An L L M extracts entities.

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:33.130
Triples merge into a graph. A clustering step finds communities.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.634
And each one gets a summary.

00:00:35.184 --> 00:00:40.386
Where the graph lives runs from plain text upward. Wikilinks first.

00:00:40.436 --> 00:00:46.974
Then NetworkX in files. Then an embedded database. A real server last.

00:00:47.524 --> 00:00:54.487
Then an evening build. Extract the typed edges. Query what connects two pages.

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:56.218
And put it in the agent's loop.

00:00:56.768 --> 00:01:01.550
Because a stale graph is worse than no graph, so it gets a weekly health check.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.146
That's the knowledge graphs handbook. Start with page one: Why Knowledge Graphs.
